    "narrative": "Today\u2019s projections highlight **Tyler Soderstrom** (69.25) and **Corbin Carroll** (67.82) as our premier offensive engines. **Tyler Soderstrom** benefits from a high-ceiling matchup against Jos\u00e9 Soriano; the combination of a favorable hitter\u2019s park and a neutral-to-positive platoon split pushes his efficiency score to the top of our board. Similarly, **Corbin Carroll** remains a must-start despite the dynamic platoon disadvantage against Connor Prielipp, as his elite speed profile\u2014critical for our scarcity-weighted strategy\u2014remains the primary driver for his high score. **Brice Turang** also earns a strong nod at 61.57; while he faces a southpaw in Mart\u00edn P\u00e9rez, the SP\u2019s shaky xERA of 4.40 and **Brice Turang**'s spot at the top of the order provide enough volume and opportunity to justify his inclusion over less favorable bench options.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the morning involved **Austin Riley**, who sits on the bench with a 46.45 score. While **Austin Riley** carries significant power upside, the optimizer penalized him heavily due to the matchup against Jacob Misiorowski\u2014a high-strikeout arm whose 2.70 xERA severely depresses the run-production ceiling. Conversely, we are keeping a close eye on the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Dalton Rushing**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Josh Naylor**, **Brice Turang**, **Max Muncy**, **Alex Freeland**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Corbin Carroll**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Travis Bazzana**. Any last-minute shifts in batting order\u2014specifically for those currently penciled into lower-third slots like **Alex Freeland** (#9) or **Kyle Karros** (#9)\u2014could force a re-evaluation of the daily cap usage. While **James Wood** provides high-upside speed, his matchup against the 3.41 xERA of Jax creates some risk, but his elite xwOBA metrics keep him locked in the starting outfield to maintain our competitive edge in the SB category.",
